The pinned bgfx allocates every Vulkan descriptor set from one shared
pool of 1024 * BGFX_CONFIG_MAX_FRAME_LATENCY sets and frees each set
individually a few frames later. In release builds VK_CHECK does not
check anything, so when a busy frame exhausts or fragments the pool
(a crowded city plus several shadow-map passes can need thousands of
sets per frame), vkAllocateDescriptorSets fails silently and a
VK_NULL_HANDLE set reaches the driver (on RADV: SIGSEGV at address 0x40
inside vkUpdateDescriptorSets, on bgfx's render thread).

This backports the approach upstream bgfx later adopted, adapted to the
pinned command queue: each command-buffer slot owns a list of descriptor
pools, allocated in chunks of 1024 sets and grown on demand (up to 64
chunks). Sets are no longer freed individually; a slot's pools are reset
in CommandQueueVK::alloc right after the wait on that slot's fence,
when none of its sets can still be in use. If every chunk is exhausted,
the allocation returns VK_NULL_HANDLE (logged once) and the draw or
dispatch is skipped instead of binding a null set.
